title The exclusion nobody could see
prompt Baton lap as BAKERY -- core infrastructure, reds first, Lindy-first and crux-first.
rota lap 4520, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears; its threshold names an honest SILENCE as a claim retired because it could no longer be checked. That is exactly what a figure written into a comment becomes once the scan beside it stops reading the population the figure describes.
think Cold roster, 228 guards: run_verdict=guard_red with two reds, and standing_equipment reads red_self off the first, so there was one -- elf_machine, sites=16 against ceiling=3, with 13 of the 16 planted in tools/fixtures/s/self_matching_assert_control.sh to prove a DIFFERENT guard counts them.
think I built the repair as a class cut -- a heredoc walk plus a per-line single-quote strip in the census awk -- and proved it by running old and new over every tracked runner: exactly one file's count changed, 13 to 0. I also built the control the census had never had, 34 behaviors on real git repositories.
think While that ran, a peer landed ed76bad18 closing the same red, better: tools/fixtures/l/live_lines.sh extracted as a sourced shell lexer shared with tools/c/convergence_census.sh, plus a git ls-files filter over _control.(sh|rish), plus nine legs added to the census's counting rule inside the existing tools/fixtures/e/elf_machine_control.sh.
think So I dropped my scan change, my control, and my witness leg whole. A second control over one census is the fleet friction this seat exists to lower, not to add to. What survives is only what measuring found missing in theirs.
obs Their filter holds out the whole _control. population -- 244 tracked files -- and prints nothing about it. tools/fixtures/p/process_reach_scan.sh states the law over its own single exclusion in its own head: an exclusion nobody can see is a claim rather than a measurement. That head also refuses a `*control*` PATTERN by name, on the ground that it blinds a guard inside every control in the tree.
obs Their head's figure went stale inside its own commit. It reads "exactly one of them carries a site", measured 20260910.035630; the same commit's other half added nine legs to elf_machine_control.sh, four of them at live positions. Read through their own lexer now: control_files=2, control_sites=8.
obs All eight are single-quoted arguments on lines that BEGIN live -- cplant '...', printf '...', case_read '...'. That is ll_live's per-line bound answering honestly, rather than a fault: it reports whether a line began inside a quoted region. So the reading is REPORTED and never gated; a gate there would red on honest fixtures, which is the shape of gate somebody turns off.
obs The counter is proven to FALL as well as to read: a leg rewrites the pen's control to plant by heredoc and asserts control_sites goes 1 to 0 while control_excluded stays 1. A counter only ever seen at one value cannot be told from a constant.
obs Two legs failed first and both were my own reading. `cout=$(csites)` ends the control under set -eu, because the pen stands over its ceiling from leg 4 and the census exits 1 there; and cfield's leading-space anchor read nothing, since control_excluded opens its own line.
obs My own dropped work carried one finding worth keeping in words: cross-line quote parity was written and measured as the alternative to a per-line walk, and it LOST a real site in tools/h/hawm1_seva_witness.rish while still leaving 8 of 13, because one apostrophe flips parity for every line after it. Losing a site is the dangerous direction. Both my reading and the peer's landed reading are per line, and that bound is now printed rather than argued.
obs elf_machine_control.sh grades C+ -- register 30, reach 70 -- and reads C+ at HEAD before my change too, since the grader scores the module head, which is a peer's prose from an hour ago and which I did not touch. Named on the card for its writer rather than rewritten under them.
